---
# Split-horizon DNS for the public auth hostnames (IN-16 stage 2).
#
# ⚠️ THIS IS NOT A COREDNS DEPLOYMENT. Deploy this chart in MANIFEST MODE
# (helm_release_enabled = false) — it renders exactly one ConfigMap and nothing else.
#
# Do NOT revive shared/helm/charts/core-dns for this. That chart deploys a whole CoreDNS and is
# deliberately commented out in init-resources because k3s manages CoreDNS itself; enabling it
# would fight k3s for ownership of the kube-system coredns/kube-dns resources. `coredns-custom` is
# k3s's own documented extension point and is left free by k3s — the Corefile already ends with
#     import /etc/coredns/custom/*.override
#     import /etc/coredns/custom/*.server
# and the Deployment already mounts this ConfigMap as an OPTIONAL volume. It simply does not exist
# until something creates it, which is what this does.
#
# WHY: the website's Auth.js BFF refreshes tokens SERVER-SIDE using the issuer URL
# (https://auth-<env>.fitmate.me). Resolved normally, that request leaves the cluster, reaches
# Cloudflare, and meets the Access login page instead of a token — so the session dies at first
# expiry while the initial login looks fine. Resolving the same name to Traefik keeps the request
# inside the cluster. The issuer STRING is unchanged, so `iss` still matches what services expect.
#
# ⚠️ This is why stage 2 also gives Traefik a real certificate. DNS alone sends the pod to a
# listener presenting TRAEFIK DEFAULT CERT, and the call dies with "unable to get local issuer
# certificate" — a NEW failure wearing the old one's clothes.
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: coredns-custom
  namespace: ${namespace}
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: terraform
data:
  # `.override` is imported INSIDE the existing `.:53` server block, so it contributes plugins to
  # the current server rather than declaring a new one. A `.server` file would take over a whole
  # zone — far more than is wanted for two names.
  auth-split-horizon.override: |
%{ for r in try(parameters.rewrites, []) ~}
    # ${r.from} -> ${r.to}
    #
    # Rewriting to the SERVICE NAME rather than a hardcoded ClusterIP means this survives Traefik's
    # Service being recreated with a different IP.
    #
    # `answer auto` rewrites the response name back to the name the client asked for. Without it the
    # answer carries the internal name, which some resolvers accept and others quietly reject —
    # a difference that only shows up under a specific client library.
    rewrite stop {
      name exact ${r.from} ${r.to}
      answer auto
    }
%{ endfor ~}
